Okay. Eight attacks on one slide looks like a lot. So don't read the cards. Read the four headers across the top.

Think about how a burglar works. First, they get in the door. Then, they get to something valuable. Then, they make sure they can come back. And last, they cover their tracks.

Attacking an AI agent works exactly the same way.

Get in. That's the first column. The attacker gets their words into something your agent reads. A wiki page. A reply from another agent.

Reach something valuable. Second column. They get to the tools your agent uses. Change what a tool says it does, and you change what the agent does.

Make it stick. Third column. They plant it in memory, or in the agent's to-do list. So it keeps working even after you've cleaned up the original problem.

And cover the tracks. Last column. The agent writes its own log. And a human approves its work based on a summary the attacker helped write. So the evidence looks fine.

Here's the important part. The attacker doesn't need all eight. They just need one from each step, and they link them together. That's the chain.

And most teams fix one column and call it done. That's like locking the front door and leaving the window open.

You'll see all three demos follow this same path. Watch for it.